ntdll: clamp oversized virtual memory reservations for Chromium / V8.

Modern Chromium and Electron (VS Code, Discord, browsers) allocate a 1.47 TB
(0x15800000000) or 512 GB (0x8000000000) MEM_RESERVE virtual memory cage for
V8 pointer compression sandbox security.

On Android ARM64 kernels built with 39-bit virtual addressing (CONFIG_ARM64_VA_BITS_39),
the entire user address space is only 512 GB. A 1.5 TB reservation request is
physically impossible to satisfy and returns STATUS_NO_MEMORY (error:
"virtual:allocate_virtual_memory out of memory for allocation, base 0x0 size 15800000000"),
killing the process immediately on startup.

V8 only uses this address space for pointer sandboxing and typically commits
between a few hundred megabytes and 4 GB of actual heap inside it.

Clamping pure MEM_RESERVE requests (> 8 GB) at base NULL to an 8 GB ceiling
allows map_view() to succeed in the available address space while preserving
normal on-demand page commits within the range.


